ane it will be the  middle of midterm exams and you will  have discovered if you haven't already  that all the other students are both  smarter and harder working than you are  now of course they think the same about  you but that doesn't help very much the  roommate you like best will be in the  midst of some terrible personal crisis  that requires your constant support the  roommate you like least we'll have  adopted some personal habit even more  annoying than what you've had to put up  with so far you will have just had your  worst game rehearsal meeting whatever it  is the partner of your dreams will have  turned you down the whole universe will  seem to be against you but it's not the  universe that is turned on you you're  just trapped in the Yale bubble so  here's one way to get out go down to  trumbull street over there block a block  down the road turn right cross Whitney  Avenue by which action you leave Yale  behind walk another block or two to  where the access to the highway is and  turn  left on Orange Street and then just keep  on walking after a mile or two you'll  get to a place called East Rock Park  it's quite a large Park there's a lake a  hill paths all around and as you wander  around it will occur to you this is  October in New England one of the most  spectacularly beautiful natural scenes  on our entire planet you will be  reminded that there are people who are  not between the ages of 18 and 22 there  are mothers with babies middle-aged  joggers old married couples some  twelve-year-old kid practicing free  throws and you'll recall that the  universe also contains non-human animals  pet pet dogs are over-represented in  east rock but there are also dear swans  fish frogs turtles songbirds of all  types walk around for an hour or so and  then head back your problems will not be  gone in fact by losing an hour of study  time they might have gotten a little bit  worse but you may find that you have a  better perspective on their importance  east rock is of course not the only way  to escape the Yale bubble if you want to  see particularly far into the rest of  the universe i recommend the lightener  observatories tuesday night open houses  up on science hill or you could go to  New York or you could call your  grandmother or you could read something  that is not assigned for a class or go  out into the community and campaign for  your favorite political party or social  cause or just shut the door close your  eyes and let your mind run free there  are more and better ways to relieve  stress then yet another late night party  with other yay Lee's who are worried  about the exact same things that you are  focusing on the inside of the bubble  ratchets up the intensity and anxiety  it's not a stress reducer at all but the  bubble walls that separate Yale from the  rest of the universe are not the only  boundaries you will encounter there are  bubbles within the Yale bubble just as  the universe has what the cosmologists  call hierarchical structure out there in  the universe galaxies form into galaxies  clusters clusters formed super clusters  clusters and the superclusters form the  edges of the bubbles and there's recent  evidence that the bubbles themselves are  grouped together and once again the  university is similar to the universe  Yale is not one undifferentiated  experience there's a wide range of sub  structure academic departments sports  teams cultural organizations performing  arts groups secret and not so secret  societies residential colleges all sorts  of smaller bubbles inside the Yale  bubble any given student inhabits just a  few of these bubbles it's why everyone's  experience of Yale is different my own  experience of Yale involved a rather  bizarre mixture of singing groups and  astrophysics courses these two bubbles  were pretty separate from one another  although they did touch on a couple of  occasions in my sophomore year I was the  pitch pipe of the Dukes men thank you  those were the days those were the days  when I was an important person around  here and we were invited to sing at the  physics department christmas party we  needed the money so i took the gig but i  have to say i found it hard to perform  our usual antics in front of people who  were going to grade my exams the very  next day people in events look different  when viewed through different bubbles  the iridescent refraction that occurs  when the edges of the bubbles collide  provides an interesting point of view  this effect occurs strongly when faculty  and students interacting unusual places  in the supermarket or the bank or  something like that I vividly remember  my first year back as a faculty member  I'd been away from Yale for a few years  and coming back I found my habits  reverting to those of my 20 year old  self I was crossing the campus late one  night perhaps eleven o'clock at p.m. and  I got thirsty and wanted a soda so just  as I would have done a decade earlier I  went into the library we called it CCL  back then but now it's been renovated  into the vast library and there used to  be a bunch of vending machines down  there that we called machine city so I  trotted down to machine city and bought  myself a coke  on the way I passed a group of students  studying and they looked at me with  absolute horror and I kind of turned  around to see what demon was following  me and then I realized these students  were in my class and I was giving a  midterm exam the very next day and they  thought I was checking up on so I  scurried away because so that they could  get on with it pretending that I hadn't  noticed them there another one of those  awkward moments when bubbles collide and  I realized that my students were now  treating me just as I had treated the  physics faculty during the Dukes mins  performance not as fellow human beings  but as some kind of alternative life  form it really shouldn't be so awkward  one of the things that makes Yale great  is the personal interaction that's  possible here between faculty and  students but it takes them real out of  the bubble behavior on both sides to  realize that promise it took me a while  to get the hang of it both as a student  and later as a faculty member but when  that connection gets made it can be  really quite wonderful so let me  encourage you if you see a faculty  member in an unexpected situation don't  freak out we're probably not checking up  on your study habits we just want a soda  just like you and a friendly gesture  might be appreciated I did know one  other person who inhabited both the  astrophysics and the singing group  bubble a young woman so spectacularly  beautiful that I couldn't speak  coherently in her presence until our  25th reunion I then found out that she's  really a very nice person and that if I  hadn't been so terrified we might have  been friends after all we did have a lot  of common interests it was a real missed  opportunity I missed many opportunities  as a student and so will you as I said  before no one can possibly do all the  things that are to be done around here  but let me describe two other mistakes I  made in the hope that it might help you  avoid a particular kind of blunder then  as now Yale had some truly spectacular  classes that no one should miss these  are generally referred to by the name of  the grand  who teach them in my day we had Spence  which was a famous course on Chinese  history and scully and even more famous  course on art history both professor  Spence and professor Scully are now  retired but I don't doubt that you  already know their contemporary  equivalents I had a strangely negative  attitude toward these courses based now  I now think on a kind of adolescent  arrogance I refuse to take any of them  on the grounds that 600 Yale students  couldn't all be right the consequence of  this though is that I now know much less  than I might about Chinese history in  the history of art both of which would  in fact provide very useful background  for some of my current activities a  second missed opportunity occurred in  graduate school not here at Yale and  relates back to those cosmic bubbles I  mentioned at the beginning the discovery  of cosmic bubbles proved to be a major  advance in astrophysics detailed studies  of hierarchical structure provides some  of the best constraints on the origin  and evolution of the universe that we  have I could have made that discovery  but I didn't one of the options I had  when choosing my PhD thesis topic was to  work on galaxy maps just the thing that  led to the discovery of cosmic structure  but this seemed to me an amazingly  boring project the plan was to observe  twenty thousand galaxies and plot them  on a map take a spectrum find the  redshift calculate the distance put it  on the map lather rinse repeat twenty  thousand times what a bore so I did  something else instead but one of my  classmates clearer thinking and more  patient than I took on the project wrote  a clever piece of software to do all the  work and the paper she wrote has been  cited more often than my top four papers  combined looking back I think these  anecdotes reveal the same flaw in both  cases I was convinced that I knew what I  was doing so much so that I refuse to  try anything outside of what had become  my comfort zone my bubble but that  bubble was really quite small and so I  missed out fortunately I got over my  silly and  Timothy toward big popular courses by my  senior year and almost accidentally took  the intro to film studies course this is  about as far from my comfort zone as  possible a big lecture course on visual  art and here I have to apologize to d  miller a distinguished art historian and  confess that this is an area in which I  have neither talent nor great interest  nevertheless taking that course was  perhaps the single best thing I did at  Yale knowing little about visual images  I had imagined that the point of going  to the movies was to find out how the  plot turned out that the content of the  film was more or less contained within  the words of the script but it turned  out greatly to my surprise that whole  realms of meaning are contained within  the images themselves who knew I did but  after taking that course I began to have  an inkling of an area of human  experience that I otherwise would have  completely missed out and this has  enriched every single one of the  embarrassingly large numbers of hours  that I've since spent watching TV so in  retrospect that course enhanced my life  more than any other academic experience  I've ever had similarly the missed  opportunity to work on galaxy maps  indicated a narrow view of what  interesting academic work might consist  of this is a flaw I'm currently trying  to push back against I've become  involved in Yale's collaboration with  the National University of Singapore to  create a new liberal arts college on the  other side of the world trying to bridge  the cultural political and intellectual  gaps between New Haven and Singapore is  an entirely new kind of challenge for me  it's a really interesting and exciting  project it's also been intensely  controversial some people think that the  nature of sync of the Singapore bubble  is such that we shouldn't be attempting  to work with them at all it's just the  kind of debate that one hopes for it a  place like Yale and that we hope will  occur in the new college in Singapore as  well one of the things that recently  happened in this project is we've hired  the first batch of faculty for this  endeavor they're fascinating group many  will be on campus here during the coming  year  and I hope that a few of them of you  will get to know them the people  attracted to this kind of thing tend to  be what the demographers call  multi-state migrants people who grew up  in one place and culture and shifted  repeatedly to other places and cultures  in other words bubble Buster's I too am  an interstate migrant but in my case the  states involved are Massachusetts in  Connecticut and somehow going from Yale  to Harvard and back doesn't really seem  all that imaginative so I'm very  grateful that in middle age I will  finally be able to do something that you  all can do in the next few years namely  study abroad it's a particularly fine  way to escape from the Yale bubble and  its various sub bubbles you know I don't  remember much from freshman week as such  in part due to activities better left  undiscussed in the presence of Dean's  but one incident has stuck with me I  remember going to a meal with two new  friends the kinds of friends that you  know you met 15 years been 15 minutes  ago and have become close buddies some  of whom I should tell you one doesn't  see again until the day before  commencement one of us noticed someone  sitting alone in the dining hall and  insisted that as a friendly gesture we  should join him to our embarrassment he  turned out not to be a freshman but an  upperclassman a junior I think quite  jaded about Yale and about life he  smiled wistfully at our youthful  enthusiasm and just shook his head AHA  youth he said looking back at us from  the advanced age of 20 and so my final  piece of advice today is this don't be  that guy hold fiercely to the sense of  excitement and adventure and anxiety  that you now have at Yale and for the  rest of your life keep having meals with  people you've never met keep taking  courses on things you know nothing about  keep trying activities you've never done  before and when you graduate don't  imagine for one minute that the course  of your life is somehow set in  don't mistake the interior of one  particular bubble for the whole of our  wide-ranging very and fascinating  University much less for the universe  itself and with that let me thank you  for your attention and welcome to Yale  Thank You professor Baylin for your  thoughtful and wise words and now as  part of this gathering we'd like to  introduce you to a long-standing Yale  tradition the singing of our alma mater  bright college years you will sing it  many times in your lives as students and  then as alumni so it is fitting that you  begin your Yale career by singing it  together for the very first time as a  class the words in tune may be  unfamiliar right now but you soon will  learn them by heart some representatives  of the Yale glee club are here today to  lead us they will sing the first verse  and then I ask that you rise and sing  both vs you've been given a Yale  handkerchief and is it is our custom to  waive it from your right to left okay on  the last line now let me now call on  Yale glee club president and morris  college resident of the class of 2013  mr. Eli Mitchell Larson and the Yale  glee club who will be accompanied on the  organ by mr. Simon Jacobs  Oh  Oh  Oh  Oh  you  you
 

